  "problemStatement": {
    "formal": "For a prime p, a unit g modulo p is a generator iff g^((p-1)/q) is not 1 for every prime q dividing p-1; generators exist in (ZMod p)^x.",
    "plain": "Backfills metadata for the primitive-root search criterion. The proof defines generator status by order, proves the standard prime-factor test for generators modulo a prime, gives an equivalent primeFactors formulation, and proves existence of a generator.",
    "whyMatters": [
      "Primitive roots turn the multiplicative group modulo a prime into a cyclic search problem.",
      "Testing only prime divisors of p-1 is the standard efficient certificate for a candidate generator.",
      "The existence theorem guarantees that the certified search has a target."
    ]
  },
  "knownResults": {
    "proven": [
      "`IsGenerator` defines primitive-root status by orderOf g = p - 1.",
      "`isGenerator_iff_prime_factor_test` proves the generator criterion over all prime divisors of p - 1.",
      "`isGenerator_iff_primeFactors_test` rewrites the test using `Nat.primeFactors`.",
      "`exists_generator` proves that the unit group modulo a prime has a generator.",
      "The listed primitive-roots files are recorded with 0 axioms and 0 sorries."
    ],
